WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.969 --> 00:00:02.418
- [Narrator] In Spring Green, Wisconsin,

00:00:02.418 --> 00:00:04.668
there is a house … on a rock.

00:00:05.946 --> 00:00:08.779
But, it is so much more than that.

00:00:11.016 --> 00:00:13.294
Inside, it is home to many bizarre

00:00:13.294 --> 00:00:15.588
and incredible collections.

00:00:15.588 --> 00:00:18.235
There's a room of
self-playing instruments,

00:00:18.235 --> 00:00:22.402
an infinity room, a giant
squid attacking a whale,

00:00:24.418 --> 00:00:27.883
and some nightmare-inducing dolls.

00:00:27.883 --> 00:00:32.261
It seems like a really strange
museum, and that's because—

00:00:32.261 --> 00:00:33.094
- No.

00:00:34.584 --> 00:00:37.438
It appears to be a museum,
'cause it is housing antiques,

00:00:37.438 --> 00:00:39.590
it's housing all kinds of memorabilia,

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:41.804
and things from a different era.

00:00:41.804 --> 00:00:44.295
It's more of a place to just enjoy.

00:00:44.295 --> 00:00:47.401
- [Narrator] So, what
is House on the Rock?

00:00:47.401 --> 00:00:50.725
- The House on the Rock, to
me, is a magical complex.

00:00:50.725 --> 00:00:53.701
It's an all around sensory explosion.

00:00:53.701 --> 00:00:54.534
- [Narrator] It was made by

00:00:54.534 --> 00:00:57.330
Alex Jordan, who was an architect?

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.517
- Not at all, not at all,
he is not a trained man.

00:01:00.517 --> 00:01:02.103
He's just a man of great vision.

00:01:02.103 --> 00:01:05.307
- [Narrator] Maybe not an
architect, but an evident hoarder.

00:01:05.307 --> 00:01:07.333
Alex Jordan, with the help of his friends,

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:09.241
built the House on the Rock in the '40s,

00:01:09.241 --> 00:01:12.280
and opened it to the public in the '60s.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.982
His collections of antiques and junk,

00:01:14.982 --> 00:01:19.192
new items and old, real
and quite obviously fake,

00:01:19.192 --> 00:01:22.911
have no central theme or
apparent reason for being there.

00:01:22.911 --> 00:01:26.726
And, maybe that's part of its charm.

00:01:26.726 --> 00:01:28.834
There are some pretty incredible things,

00:01:28.834 --> 00:01:32.438
including a huge, unrideable carousel.

00:01:32.438 --> 00:01:36.102
- 269 different animals,
not one of them is a horse.

00:01:36.102 --> 00:01:36.935
- [Narrator] Really?

00:01:36.935 --> 00:01:39.992
- Yes, the horses adorn the walls.

00:01:39.992 --> 00:01:42.152
- [Narrator] Of course they do.

00:01:42.152 --> 00:01:43.717
- [Jennifer] You know, every
time you turn the corner here,

00:01:43.717 --> 00:01:45.562
it's a different thing
and it's a different wow,

00:01:45.562 --> 00:01:48.675
and you don't know what to expect.

00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:49.971
- [Narrator] One man's trash is

00:01:49.971 --> 00:01:53.471
another man's roadside tourist attraction.

